When composing strings for use with the 'S' and 'R' commands or for the 'i', 'a',
'e', 'd', '>', and '<' commands in <string> mode, there are a few things to keep
in mind.

First, if your strings do not include spaces or do not start with a double quote
('"'), a period and a dash ('.-'), an at symbol ('@'), or a number, then there is
no problem.  In any of these cases, your string MUST start with a double quote.

If a string is started with a double quote, the end of the string if marked by
the end of your input, a double quote at the end of your input, or a double quote
followed by a space.  If you want the trailing quote and the space to be a part
of the string, you must precede the space with a backslash ('\').  If you want
the backslash to be included, use a double backslash (`\\`).  If you want both
backslashes, use a trible backslash ('\\\'), and so forth.  In any other case,
the backslash and the double quotes have no special meaning and will
automatically be made part of the string.

Examples:
---------
Literal input:      Resulting string(s): (without single quotes)
--------------      --------------------
"abc                'abc'
"abc"               'abc'
"abc "              'abc '
""abc "             '"abc '
"abc" def"          'abc' and 'def"'
"abc"\ def"         'abc" def'
"abc"\\ def"        'abc"\ def'
"abc"\\\ def"       'abc"\\ def'
"abc\ def"          'abc\ def'
""                  empty string
a"bc                'a"bc'
\abc                '\abc'
